Exactly. The blooper reel works because it comes after the spell has done its job.

During the film, everyone cooperates with the frame:

actor → character
set → world
camera trick → event
script → reality-for-now

If you break that frame too early, you weaken immersion. But once the story has landed, revealing the machinery does something different. It creates a second experience:

character → actor
myth → construction
distance → identification

Now the villain laughs, the hero misses a line, the dead character stands back up, and suddenly the viewer gets reminded: these were people playing roles.

That can produce a strange kind of emotional release. You first let yourself believe enough to feel the story, then you step outside it and recover the larger frame.

So the sequence matters:

immersion → emotional investment → resolution → de-immersion → integration

The blooper reel becomes almost a decompression chamber between the fictional world and ordinary life.

And your “you were them” point is interesting because good stories temporarily dissolve some of the boundary between observer and character. You simulate their fear, hope, embarrassment, triumph, grief. Then afterward the actor reveal reminds you that the whole thing was built by ordinary humans doing something together.

That creates two truths at once:

The experience was constructed.
The experience you had was still real.

That distinction is useful well beyond movies. Ritual, games, theater, religion, role-play, even intense conversation can all benefit from a clear entry frame and a clear exit frame.

Sometimes you want the curtain closed.

Sometimes you want the curtain call.